Included in SAMPLES.ZIP there are some sample BOARD.BAT and BOARD.CMD files.
These look very similar to what has been used in the past, but there are some
significant differences which may surprise you.

   If your board suddenly lands at a DOS prompt, or if the window closes
   at the end of a door or event, then this section should be re-read.

The changes to BOARD.CMD are out of necessity, due to the way OS/2 handles
calling .BAT files from within an OS/2 session.  The changes made to BOARD.BAT
are nearly identical, and were made simply for consistency.  If you run both
DOS and OS/2 versions, then updating your BOARD.BAT file to this new standard
will help you to set up the OS/2 version in a way that keeps both versions
working the same way using the same files, and that'll save some hair. <g>

- PCB-OS2 should be run from a BOARD.CMD which is structured nearly identical
  to the BOARD.BAT file.  Examples of the BOARD.BAT and BOARD.CMD files follow:

    BOARD.BAT
    ---------
    @echo off
    :top
    c:
    cd\pcb
    if exist remote.bat REN remote.bat remote.sys
    if exist door.bat   DEL door.bat
    if exist endpcb     DEL endpcb
    pcboardm
    if exist remote.bat CALL remote.bat
    if exist door.bat   CALL door.bat
    if exist event.bat  CALL event.bat
    if NOT exist endpcb GOTO top
    :end

    BOARD.CMD
    ---------
    @echo off
    :top
    c:
    cd\pcb
    set dszlog=pcbdsz.log
    if exist remote.cmd REN remote.cmd remote.sys
    if exist endpcb     DEL endpcb
    pcboard2
    if exist remote.cmd CALL remote.cmd
    if exist event.cmd  CALL event.cmd
    if exist event.bat  CALL event.bat
    if NOT exist endpcb GOTO top
    :end

  NOTE: Inside PCBCP.DOC you can find a slightly modified version of BOARD.CMD
        which makes it easy to set up multiple nodes sharing a single copy of
        the board.cmd file and a single copy of the pcboard.dat file by
        utilizing a startup file which simply sets some environment variables.

  NOTE: The above BOARD.BAT file requires that you be using DOS version 3.3 or
        later.  Of course, if you're running a DOS window under OS/2, that will
        work as well.  Specifically, the CALL statement was not added to DOS
        until version 3.3.

- There are differences in the way OS/2 handles calls to .BAT files from within
  a .CMD file as compared to how DOS handles a call to a .BAT file from within
  another .BAT file.  These differences could complicate your doors and events
  if you are running both DOS and OS/2 versions of PCBoard because the .BAT
  files would need to work one way for DOS and another way for OS/2.

  To help simplify the matter, the sample BOARD.BAT and BOARD.CMD files shown
  above have some changes in them.  Specifically, the usage of the CALL
  statement, and the method of recycling the batch file.

  The reasons for adding the CALL statement are as follows:

  1) It means that you can now REMOVE any reference to BOARD.BAT from your
     door batch files.  The reason is that under DOS, you'll want to return
     back to BOARD.BAT but under OS/2 you'll want to return to BOARD.CMD.

     NOTE:  If you are running both DOS and OS/2 nodes, then your BOARD.BAT
            file should receive the same treatment.  Go thru the BOARD.BAT
            file and add CALL to each of the lines that transfer control to
            a batch file (see the BOARD.BAT file above as an example).

  2) Another reason why this change is required is that, under DOS when you
     execute a batch file, it transfers control to that batch file and it
     never comes back.  But if an OS/2 .CMD file executes a .BAT file, it will
     open up a window to run the .BAT file and then it *will* come back when
     its done (as if a CALL statement had been used).  So to resolve the
     differences and have both DOS and OS/2 work the same way, it is best to
     explicitly use a CALL statement.  That way you can be sure that it will
     always come back to BOARD.BAT or BOARD.CMD as appropriate.

  NOTE: Since the DOOR batch files are going to be CALL'ed, in order to be
        consistent, the sample BOARD.BAT and BOARD.CMD files have been modified
        so that the rest of the batch files use CALL statements to run them.
        This means that you should edit your REMOTE.SYS file as well as your
        EVENT batch files and remove the reference to BOARD.BAT.

  Another change you may have noticed is that the BOARD.CMD file no longer
  tests for DOOR.BAT.  This is because the OS/2 version of PCBoard runs all
  doors as "shelled" doors.  So PCBoard creates the door.bat and then shells
  out to run it (instead of "exiting to dos" and letting the board.bat file or
  board.cmd control execution of the door).

  NOTE:  PCBoard runs either a DOOR.BAT or a DOOR.CMD file, depending on
         whether or not you put a Y in the OS/2 column of the DOORS.LST file.

  Also, you'll note that there are TWO event lines in the batch file.  One
  which checks for the existence of EVENT.CMD and another to check for the
  existence of EVENT.BAT.  This is because you can set a Y/N flag in the
  event editor to indicate if an event is an OS/2 or DOS event.  PCBoard then
  creates the event batch file of the appropriate type (BAT or CMD) and exits
  to allow BOARD.CMD run the batch file.

  The last change that was made is not so much out of necessity, as it is for
  simplication.  In the past, BOARD.BAT would check for the existence of
  ENDPCB and would exit if ENDPCB existed.  The above example instead checks
  for the NON-existence of ENDPCB and if it does NOT exist, it simply jumps to
  the top of the BOARD.CMD file and resumes execution.

  NOTE:  This means that you would need to remove the call to BOARD.BAT from
         the end of an existing BOARD.BAT file.

  The advantage to this change is that it means your door batch files, your
  event batch files, and your remote.sys file no longer need to change the
  drive or directory because right after the :top statement, the drive and
  directory are properly set by BOARD.CMD.  Keeping the drive and directory
  changes in your batch files won't hurt anything, but setting them wrong will
  no longer mean that you will accidently return back to the wrong node!
